  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & \u22481+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
